    "text": "<<Sniffing a Flower Explained to 3rd Graders>>\n\nWhen you sniff a flower, you are using your nose to feel the smell. The flower has chemicals in it that make it smell good. These chemicals are called **smells** or **odors**. When you breathe in, the chemicals go into your nose and travel to your brain. Your brain then tells you what the smell is. \n\nFlowers have different smells because each flower has its own special chemicals. Some flowers smell like sweet candy, others like fresh air, and some like strong perfume. When you sniff a flower, you are learning about the different smells that nature has to offer.",
